To compute the current speed of an object in a C program, we typically need to know the object's velocity over time. Velocity is the rate of change of an object's position with respect to time. Here's a simple C program that calculates the average speed of an object moving along a one-dimensional axis (let's say in meters per second if the units are consistent).

This program will assume that the object starts at position `pos1` and ends at `pos2`, and the time interval `delta_t` is provided. The average speed is calculated using the formula:

\[ \text{Average Speed} = \frac{\Delta \text{Position}}{\Delta \text{Time}} \]

Here's the C program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

// Function to calculate average speed
double calculate_average_speed(double pos1, double pos2, double delta_t) {
    // Calculate the change in position (displacement)
    double displacement = pos2 - pos1;

    // Calculate the average speed
    double average_speed = displacement / delta_t;

    return average_speed;
}

